  "content": "\\section{Introduction}\n\nGlobal logistics networks face mounting pressure to reduce their environmental footprint while maintaining operational efficiency. As climate change concerns intensify, the transportation sector, which accounts for approximately 24\\% of global CO$_2$ emissions from fuel combustion \\cite{peng2020promoting}, has become a critical target for sustainability initiatives. Within this context, the Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) has emerged as a promising paradigm that leverages advanced digital technologies to create more efficient and sustainable logistics systems \\cite{wu2024cyber}.\n\nThe concept of the Physical Internet (PI), first proposed conceptually in The Economist \\cite{economist2006pi} and later formalized by Montreuil \\cite{montreuil2011toward}, aims to establish an open global logistics system based on physical, digital, and operational interconnectivity. Building upon this foundation, the Cyber-Physical Internet strengthens the synergistic use of cyber capabilities to improve logistics operations through enhanced information synchronization and decision-making \\cite{wu2024cyber}. In CPI networks, physical assets are monitored and managed through sophisticated digital systems that enable real-time optimization and control.\n\n\\subsection{Problem Statement}\n\nDespite significant advancements in logistics optimization, current CPI routing protocols predominantly focus on either time or cost minimization, with carbon emission considerations often treated as secondary concerns or overlooked entirely. Recent research has begun to address this gap through the development of carbon-aware routing protocols \\cite{ng2024carbon}, which prioritize environmental impact in routing decisions. However, these protocols typically rely on static optimization based on predefined parameters and often fail to adapt to changing traffic conditions in real-time.\n\nThis limitation creates a critical gap in sustainable logistics management: the inability to dynamically adjust carbon-optimized routes in response to real-world traffic variability. When traffic conditions change unexpectedly—as they frequently do in complex urban environments—static routing strategies can lead to suboptimal outcomes from both environmental and operational perspectives. Vehicles may become trapped in congestion, resulting in increased idling time, higher fuel consumption, and consequently elevated carbon emissions, while also compromising delivery schedules.\n\n\\subsection{Research Questions}\n\nTo address this significant gap in sustainable logistics routing, our research investigates the following key questions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item How can a carbon-aware routing protocol be designed to incorporate real-time traffic information effectively?\n    \\item What framework architecture is needed to seamlessly integrate carbon metrics and traffic data in CPI networks?\n    \\item How does the performance of carbon-aware adaptive routing compare to static carbon-aware routing in terms of emissions reduction and operational efficiency under various traffic conditions?\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{Proposed Approach}\n\nThis paper presents a novel Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for CPI networks that addresses the limitations of existing approaches. Our solution integrates carbon emission optimization with real-time traffic adaptation through a comprehensive two-layer framework, as illustrated in Figure~\\ref{fig:framework}.\n\nThe proposed framework consists of two complementary layers:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Link Layer}: Manages the organization of autonomous logistics areas and their interconnections, incorporating real-time traffic data collection points. This layer continuously gathers and processes traffic information from various sources, maintains an up-to-date representation of the transportation network, and stores current and historical traffic conditions.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Transport Layer}: Handles routing decisions based on carbon metrics and real-time traffic information. This layer computes carbon emissions for different routes, continuously evaluates routing decisions when significant traffic changes are detected, and balances carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints using a weighted objective function.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nAt the core of our approach is a multi-objective optimization algorithm that dynamically balances carbon emissions and travel time through a weighted objective function. This algorithm normalizes and combines these competing objectives, allowing logistics operators to adjust the relative importance based on their specific sustainability goals and operational requirements.\n\n\\subsection{Key Contributions}\n\nThis research makes several significant contributions to the field of sustainable logistics routing in CPI networks:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item We develop a novel two-layer architecture that seamlessly integrates carbon emission metrics with real-time traffic data, enabling adaptive routing decisions that consider both environmental impact and operational efficiency.\n    \n    \\item We design and implement a Carbon-Aware Adaptive routing algorithm that uses a multi-objective approach to balance carbon emissions and travel time, providing a more comprehensive solution than existing single-objective approaches.\n    \n    \\item We create a comprehensive carbon emission calculation methodology that accounts for various factors affecting transportation-related emissions, including vehicle type, load, speed efficiency, and distance.\n    \n    \\item We conduct extensive experimental evaluation using a simulation of the Greater Bay Area transportation network, demonstrating the effectiveness of our approach across different traffic scenarios, vehicle types, and routing requirements.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nOur experimental results reveal that the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol achieves a balanced performance across multiple metrics. In mixed traffic conditions, which most closely represent real-world operations, our approach achieves the best balance between travel time and emissions awareness, with the lowest average travel time (75.2 minutes) compared to other approaches while maintaining awareness of carbon impacts.\n\nImportantly, the statistical analysis confirms that the difference in travel time between our Carbon-Aware Adaptive algorithm and the purely Traffic-Adaptive algorithm is not statistically significant (p = 0.3872), suggesting that our approach achieves comparable time performance while also optimizing for carbon emissions—a critical advancement for sustainable logistics operations.\n\n\\subsection{Paper Organization}\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 provides a comprehensive review of related work in CPI routing optimization, carbon-aware routing protocols, real-time traffic integration, and CPI frameworks for logistics. Section 3 details our methodology, including the two-layer architecture, carbon-aware routing algorithms, carbon emission calculation methodology, and simulation environment. Section 4 presents the experimental results and analysis, examining the performance of different routing strategies across various traffic scenarios, vehicle types, and routes. Finally, Section 5 concludes the paper with a summary of contributions, discussion of practical implications, acknowledgment of limitations, and suggestions for future research directions.",
